Id: lin046i
Severity: INFO
Category: kernel
Check: check_sysctl
Fix: Set fs.protected_regular=2 in /etc/sysctl.d.
References: Linux kernel sysctl documentation (admin-guide/sysctl/fs, protected_regular); sysctl.d(5)
Controls: CIS 4.1, 10.5; NIST CM-6, SI-16; ISO 8.9; CE Secure configuration

fs.protected_regular is 1: a program that creates a file by name in a
world-writable sticky directory (/tmp) is refused when a regular file
of that name, owned by another user, is already there, so it cannot
be made to write into that user's file. 2 extends the rule to
group-writable sticky directories, where a member of the group can
plant the file. Debian and Ubuntu ship 2 (procps'
99-protect-links.conf), Fedora and Arch 1 (systemd's
50-default.conf), Alpine the kernel's 0 (lin031w). A hardening
opportunity: Lynis asks for 2, the CIS Benchmarks do not name the
key.
  # sysctl -w fs.protected_regular=2
